<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uclés</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ucles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uclés is a village in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuenca-spain">Cuenca, Spain</a>. In the 11th century it was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhul-nunid-dynasty">Banu Dhi al-Nun</a>, later lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, and in 1108 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> defeated a Castilian army there, killing Sancho, the only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>. From 1174 it was the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a>, whose great monastery, rebuilt in the 16th to 18th centuries, dominates the village.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ucles.jpg" alt="Uclés" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Turismo en la Manchuela · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ucl%C3%A9s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
